# --- s022 Book I d.3 q.7 (conservation of the species: no corresponding question in Vatican Ord. I d.3)
n("n418","s022-01","Septima questio tertie distinctionis","la","commentary","There is no separate question on the conservation of the intelligible species in the Vatican *Ordinatio* I d. 3. There the point is made only in the closing paragraph of p. 3 q. 1 (n. 400: *nisi ponatur eam posse conservare speciem intelligibilem praeter actum*). Gorris's text, with its own arguments and *ad oppositum*, therefore reflects either a question present in his exemplar of the *Ordinatio* (the early printed tradition counts more questions in d. 3, cf. his 'eleven' at s016) or material drawn from elsewhere in Scotus, e.g. *Ord.* IV d. 45 q. 2, on memory in the separated soul. The question needs to be identified in the incunable editions of the *Ordinatio* (Venice 1477/1481).","to-verify")
n("n419","s022-01","⟨Ari⟩stoteles","la","textual","Stain at the top right of p. 38. Supplied; the reading is certain from the remaining *stoteles*.","certain")
n("n420","s022-01","commentator vocat memoriam arcam specierum","la","source","Cf. Arist. *De anima* III 4, 429a27–28 (the soul as *locus specierum*, praised in the ancients, cited by Scotus at *Ord.* I d. 3 n. 342) and Averroes, *In De an.* III comm. 6. *Arca/archa specierum* as a name for memory is Augustinian–Avicennian in flavour (Avicenna, *De anima* IV 1: memory as *thesaurus* of intentions). I have not found the exact phrase in Averroes. In ¶3 Gorris ascribes the same dictum to Aristotle.","to-verify")
n("n421","s022-02","opinio Auicenne","la","source","Avicenna, *Liber de anima* V 6: the human intellect does not retain intelligible forms when it is not actually understanding; it must turn again to the agent Intelligence to receive them. Scotus discusses the view at *Ord.* IV d. 45 q. 2. A *dicentis quod debet poni species* seems to need a negative (*non debet*), or else refers to the species abstracted from sensibles, which, on the interpretation reported next, does remain.","certain")
n("n422","s022-03","Intellectus inquantum intellectus potest esse sapiens de se","la","source","Cf. Arist. *De anima* III 4, 429b5–9: once the intellect has become each thing, as the knower actually is, it can then act of itself (*potest operari per se ipsum*). The interpretation of Aristotle's text in terms of retained species is standard in the Scotist school.","certain")
n("n423","s022-03","necesse est intelligentem fantasmata speculari","la","source","Arist. *De anima* III 8, 432a8–9 (*necesse est quemcumque intelligere phantasmata speculari*). Gorris's gloss, *vel speculatum fuisse* ('or to have contemplated them'), limits Aristotle's requirement of a phantasm to the original acquisition of the species.","certain")
n("n424","s022-03","finitas in infinitum","la","commentary","An application of the Aristotelian distinction between actual and potential infinity (*Phys.* III 6): the memory holds a finite number of species at any time, which can be increased without limit.","certain")
# --- B collation (full): B35L–B35R
n("n1100","s022-02","dicentis quod debet poni species. Sed aliqui exponunt Auicennam quod","la","textual","B omits these words and reads *ponitur opinio Auicenne qui sic intellexit quod duplex est species*. B thus gives the two-species account as Avicenna's own view, not as an interpretation of him.","certain")
n("n1101","s022-03","nam est textus. 3°. de anima","la","textual","B: *Nam est textus de anima* (without *3°.*).","certain")
